# REMAP-Net: Recursive Epistemic Meta-Abstraction with Plasticity

[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-blue.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)
[![Python 3.10+](https://img.shields.io/badge/python-3.10+-blue.svg)](https://www.python.org/downloads/)
[![PyTorch 2.0+](https://img.shields.io/badge/PyTorch-2.0+-ee4c2c.svg)](https://pytorch.org/)

## Abstract
**REMAP-Net** represents a novel paradigm in deep learning architectures, moving beyond static weights towards a dynamic, self-modifying system. By structuring learning as a three-tier recursive hierarchy ($F_0$, $F_1$, $F_2$), REMAP-Net actively models its own learning process, estimating both plasticity (how to change) and epistemic uncertainty (when and where to change). Coupled with a Stability Guardian (based on Lyapunov theory), an Abstractive-Associative Filter (AAF) for Information Bottleneck compression, and an Epistemic Memory module, it mitigates catastrophic forgetting while achieving state-of-the-art sample efficiency in non-stationary and few-shot environments.

## Architecture Diagram
```mermaid
graph TD
    X[Input Data] --> AAF[Abstractive-Associative Filter]
    AAF --> F0[F0: Base Neural Network]
    
    F0 --> Output[Predictions]
    Output --> Loss[Loss Calculation]
    
    Loss --> F1[F1: Meta-Plasticity]
    F1 -.->|Modulates Gradients| F0
    
    F1 --> F2[F2: Epistemic Recursion]
    F2 -.->|Updates F1 Params| F1
    
    AAF <--> Mem[Epistemic Memory]
    
    SG[Stability Guardian] -.->|Projects| F0
    SG -.->|Projects| F1
```

## Installation

### Prerequisites
- Python 3.10+
- PyTorch 2.0+ (CUDA recommended)

### Setup
```bash
# Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/your-username/remap-net.git
cd remap-net

# Create a virtual environment
python -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate  # On Windows: venv\Scripts\activate

# Install dependencies
pip install -e .
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

## Quick Start

### 1. Download Datasets
```bash
python scripts/download_data.py --dataset omniglot
```

### 2. Training
To train the model on Omniglot (Few-shot setting):
```bash
python -m remap_net.main --config experiments/few_shot_omniglot.yaml --output_dir results/omniglot
```

### 3. Evaluation
To evaluate a trained model:
```bash
python -m remap_net.evaluate --checkpoint results/omniglot/best_model.pth --config experiments/few_shot_omniglot.yaml
```

## Project Structure
```text
remap-net/
├── remap_net/                  # Core library
│   ├── core/                   # Main architectural components
│   │   ├── abstraction.py      # Abstractive-Associative Filter
│   │   ├── epistemic.py        # F2: Epistemic Recursion
│   │   ├── memory.py           # Epistemic Memory
│   │   ├── meta_plasticity.py  # F1: Meta-Plasticity Network
│   │   ├── remapping.py        # Main ReMAPNet integration
│   │   └── stability.py        # Stability Guardian
│   ├── models/                 # Backbones (F0)
│   ├── utils/                  # Helper utilities
│   └── visualization/          # IEEE-grade plotting tools
├── experiments/                # YAML configuration files
├── scripts/                    # Scripts for data, ablation, plots
├── tests/                      # Pytest suite
└── README.md
```

## Configuration Guide
The framework uses YAML for configuration. See `experiments/few_shot_omniglot.yaml` for a complete example. 
- **backbone**: Defines the $F_0$ architecture.
- **meta_plasticity**: $F_1$ settings including low-rank dimensions.
- **epistemic**: $F_2$ settings (SVGD particles).
- **stability**: Lyapunov guardian hyperparameters.

## Benchmark Reproduction
To run the full ablation suite:
```bash
python scripts/run_ablation.py --config experiments/ablation_full.yaml --seeds 42 43 44
```

To generate the LaTeX tables and IEEE-styled plots for the paper:
```bash
python scripts/generate_tables.py
python scripts/generate_plots.py
```

